<BaseLayout
  title="BPC-157: What the Research Actually Shows — Nawtch"
  description="BPC-157 is unapproved, restricted for compounding, and WADA-banned — the real animal vs. human evidence and current legal status."
  canonicalPath="/supplements/peptides/bpc-157/"
  bodyClass="diets"
>
      <header>
        <div class="header-left">
          <h1>BPC-157</h1>
          <p class="subtitle">Never approved, restricted for compounding, and banned in competitive sport.</p>
        </div>
        <a class="ghost-btn" href="/supplements/peptides/">← Back to Peptides</a>
      </header>

      <div class="settings-section" style="border:1px solid var(--accent); background:rgba(200,240,96,0.08);">
        <h2 class="settings-section-title" style="color:var(--accent);">Last Verified: August 14, 2026</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="settings-section" style="border:1px solid var(--accent3); background:rgba(240,160,96,0.08);">
        <h2 class="settings-section-title" style="color:var(--accent3);">Tier 2 — Investigational / Not Currently Approved</h2>
        <div class="settings-section-body">
          <p class="settings-desc">
            BPC-157 has some published research behind it, but it is <strong>not FDA-approved for
            any use</strong>, and human clinical trial data is extremely limited. This page
            describes what's actually known — <strong>not dosing or usage guidance, and not a
            recommendation to use it.</strong> Talk to a doctor before considering it.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="settings-section">
        <h2 class="settings-section-title">What It Is</h2>
        <div class="settings-section-body">
          <p class="settings-desc">
            BPC-157 ("Body Protection Compound-157") is a synthetic 15-amino-acid peptide
            fragment, originally sequenced from a protective protein identified in human gastric
            juice. Proposed mechanisms — based on animal research — include promoting
            angiogenesis (new blood vessel formation), modulating nitric-oxide signaling, and
            reducing inflammation, which is why it's marketed around tissue and tendon repair.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="settings-section">
        <h2 class="settings-section-title">What Research Actually Exists</h2>
        <div class="settings-section-body">
          <p class="settings-desc">
            The evidence base is overwhelmingly animal research — rat and mouse studies on gut,
            tendon, and wound healing. Human data is genuinely minimal: a small 2025 safety pilot
            gave an intravenous dose to just 2 healthy adults with no adverse effects reported,
            and a couple of other very small studies (roughly a dozen or so patients each) have
            looked at it for bladder pain and knee pain. There is no completed large-scale human
            clinical trial. This is a real research gap, not a technicality — claims of "proven
            safety" or "proven efficacy" in humans go well beyond what this evidence actually
            supports.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="settings-section">
        <h2 class="settings-section-title">Current Legal &amp; Regulatory Status</h2>
        <div class="settings-section-body">
          <p class="settings-desc">
            BPC-157 has never been FDA-approved for any human use. Its compounding status has
            shifted more than once in 2026: the FDA had restricted it from compounding pharmacies
            (its "Category 2" bulk substances list, citing safety-data and impurity concerns), and
            that restriction was lifted effective April 22, 2026 when the nomination was
            withdrawn. On July 23, 2026, the FDA's Pharmacy Compounding Advisory Committee voted
            8-6 (with one abstention) to recommend adding BPC-157 to the list of substances
            compounding pharmacies are formally permitted to use — but a favorable advisory vote is
            a recommendation, not a rule change: compounding pharmacies cannot legally use BPC-157
            under Section 503A until the FDA completes formal rulemaking, a process that typically
            runs 8–24 months. In short: no FDA drug approval either way, and even the more limited
            question of legal compounding access is still pending formal action. See our <a href="/supplements/peptides/">Peptides hub page</a> for the fuller regulatory picture across this
            group of compounds.
            <br /><br />
            It's also prohibited at all times in competitive sport under the World Anti-Doping
            Agency's Prohibited List.
          </p>
          <br />
          <a href="https://www.fda.gov/drugs/human-drug-compounding/bulk-drug-substances-used-compounding-under-section-503a-fdc-act" target="_blank" rel="noopener">FDA — Bulk Drug Substances Used in Compounding →</a>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="settings-section">
        <h2 class="settings-section-title">Known &amp; Theoretical Risks</h2>
        <div class="settings-section-body">
          <p class="settings-desc">
            Because robust human safety data doesn't exist, real risks — immune reactions,
            impurity-related harms, long-term effects — are essentially unstudied in people rather
            than ruled out. "Generally regarded as safe" marketing claims are not backed by the
            kind of human toxicology data that supports that phrase for an approved drug.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="settings-section">
        <h2 class="settings-section-title">Learn More</h2>
        <div class="settings-section-body">
          <a href="https://www.fda.gov/drugs/human-drug-compounding/bulk-drug-substances-used-compounding-under-section-503a-fdc-act" target="_blank" rel="noopener">FDA — Bulk Drug Substances Used in Compounding Under Section 503A</a>
        </div>
      </div>
</BaseLayout>
